course: dictionary
lesson: 2905
title: Downplaying: making things sound smaller
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To downplay something means to make it seem smaller, less important or less serious than it really is. «It's just a scratch» is a common example: it makes a deep cut sound harmless, even though it may need pressure and an adult.
explain: You can also downplay your own success. Downplaying your success means talking about it modestly, so you do not seem to show off. «I may have slightly broken the window» is downplaying to avoid trouble; the honest «I broke the window» is better.
explain: Downplaying is the opposite of exaggerating. Exaggerating makes something sound bigger than it is, and downplaying makes it sound smaller. Both change the truth, so check the real facts before you believe either one.
explain: Never downplay a real injury or danger. If someone says «I'm fine, it's just a scratch» about a cut that won't stop bleeding, check it and tell an adult.
fact: To downplay means to make something seem smaller, less important or less serious than it really is.
fact: Downplaying is also called understatement.
fact: «It's just a scratch» about a deep cut is downplaying a real injury.
fact: Downplaying your own success means talking about it modestly, so you do not seem to show off.
fact: When a real problem is downplayed, it is wise to check the facts and tell an adult.
exercise: «It's just a scratch» about a cut that won't stop bleeding. What is the speaker doing? | Asking for a plaster for a joke | Exaggerating how big the cut is | *Downplaying a real injury | Telling the exact truth about the cut
exercise: «I may have slightly broken the window.» Why might someone say this? | *To avoid trouble, by making it sound smaller | To make the window sound bigger | To show how strong they are | To tell a funny story
exercise: What does it mean to downplay your own success? | Make it sound much bigger than it was | Shout about it to everyone you know | *Talk about it modestly, so you do not seem to show off | Forget that it happened
test: Which word is the opposite of downplaying? | Whispering | Hiding | Shrinking | *Exaggerating
test: Someone says «I'm fine» about a real injury. What should you do? | Never mind, it is just a scratch | Wait and see whether it goes away | *Check it and tell an adult | Keep it to yourself so nobody worries
exam: In a news story, a leader calls a problem «only a small hiccup» when it affects thousands of people. What should a careful reader do? | Believe it, because leaders always tell the truth | *Ask whether the problem is worse than they say, and check the facts | Decide the problem must be funny | Stop reading the news
